---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="Pavel Kleisner - Independent Senior Backend Engineer & Team Lead">

    <section class="hero bleed">
        <div>
            <h1>Pavel Kleisner</h1>
            <p class="role">Independent senior backend engineer and team lead, working remotely from near Prague.</p>
            <p class="lead">I build and look after the backend systems that real businesses run on, and I stay around for what happens after the deploy.</p>
            <div class="body">
                <p>Nine years of Kotlin, Java, Spring and PostgreSQL. I started with complete features in an enterprise Java app, moved to full-stack work and today I lead production rollouts, integrations and incident analysis on a telco service platform.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="actions">
                <a href="/projects" class="btn primary">Experience and projects</a>
                <a href="mailto:contact@pavelkleisner.dev">contact@pavelkleisner.dev</a>
            </div>
        </div>
        <figure class="portrait">
            <img src="/pavel.jpg" alt="Pavel Kleisner" width="600" height="750" />
            <figcaption>Near Prague, CET.</figcaption>
        </figure>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>What I usually work on</h2>
        <div class="tiles">
            <div class="tile">
                <h3>Integrations</h3>
                <p>B2B APIs, message contracts, process flows, retries and the failure modes that only show up once a partner system is connected.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="tile">
                <h3>Releases</h3>
                <p>New platform versions into real environments: old data, backward compatibility, rollout order and a way back. I led the TSM 2.2 and 2.4 rollouts.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="tile">
                <h3>Debugging production</h3>
                <p>Logs, metrics, the database and domain knowledge together. I look for the cause, not for a patch that makes the symptom go quiet.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="tile">
                <h3>Architecture</h3>
                <p>Small safe changes, clearly named rules and sensible service boundaries. As little ceremony as the problem allows.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="feature bleed">
        <div>
            <h2>How I work with AI</h2>
            <p class="quote">AI writes a lot of code with me. It doesn't make decisions for me.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="prose">
            <p>I use AI tools every day and I've put real time into using them well: agents that dig through an unfamiliar codebase, draft the boring parts, run the tests, prepare a migration or review a diff before I do. It makes me noticeably faster and lets me take on work I wouldn't have had time for.</p>
            <p>What it doesn't change is who is responsible. I read and understand every line that goes out under my name, I know why it is there and what it touches, and when something breaks in production, the analysis and the fix are still mine.</p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section story">
        <div>
            <p class="label">A story from production</p>
            <h2>The UUID that wasn't a UUID</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="prose">
            <p>When we rolled out the first platform version, the integration spec said the <code>messageId</code> would be a UUID. One partner started failing straight away. Their system had been quietly parsing the ticket ID out of the old <code>ticketId_timestamp</code> format, even though the ticket ID sat right there in the message body.</p>
            <p>The clean fix was on their side, but in a corporate setting that change would have taken months. So I switched our side back to the old shape and production kept running. Sometimes the right call isn't the pure one. It's the one that doesn't break someone else at 2 a.m.</p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>Where I've worked</h2>
        <ol class="timeline">
            <li>
                <span class="when">2020 – now</span>
                <div class="what"><b>TSM / CETIN</b>
                    <p>Senior backend and integration engineer, team lead. Kotlin and Java services on PostgreSQL, B2B flows, change management, incident analysis and the rollouts of versions 2.2 and 2.4.</p></div>
            </li>
            <li>
                <span class="when">2018 – 2021</span>
                <div class="what"><b>MNCP</b>
                    <p>Full-stack engineer. Kotlin/Spring, Angular and database work: authentication, exports, archives and measurement batches.</p></div>
            </li>
            <li>
                <span class="when">2017 – 2018</span>
                <div class="what"><b>ZIS</b>
                    <p>Enterprise Java. ZK/ZUL screens, services and DAO layers, imports, Excel processing. Where I learned to deliver a feature end to end.</p></div>
            </li>
        </ol>
        <p class="muted" style="margin-top:1.2rem">All of it in a long-term collaboration with DataLite since 2017, the last several years as an independent contractor. More on the <a href="/projects">projects page</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>Stack</h2>
        <div class="stack-rows">
            <div><h3>Every day</h3><p>Kotlin, Java, Spring, PostgreSQL, Kafka</p></div>
            <div><h3>Also</h3><p>Kubernetes, Oracle, Angular, Python and Django, TypeScript, Astro</p></div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="cta bleed">
        <h2>Need a senior engineer for backend systems, integrations or production releases?</h2>
        <p>Long-term or focused engagements, remotely from near Prague. Email is the fastest way to reach me.</p>
        <a class="email" href="mailto:contact@pavelkleisner.dev">contact@pavelkleisner.dev</a>
    </section>

</Layout>
